 "script": [
  {
   "id": "hook",
   "text": "The oceans hold more heat than ever measured. And El Niño is tapping it."
  },
  {
   "id": "f1",
   "text": "The World Meteorological Organization released its update as COP thirty-one opened. It says that from January to September, the planet averaged about one point four seven degrees above pre-industrial levels. That puts twenty twenty-six on track to be among the four warmest years on record."
  },
  {
   "id": "f2",
   "text": "Sea level hit a record too. The W.M.O. says June, July and August saw the highest global sea level since satellite measurements began in nineteen ninety-three. It says ocean heat set a record in twenty twenty-five and kept rising this year."
  },
  {
   "id": "f3",
   "text": "W.M.O. chief Celeste Saulo says El Niño releases heat stored in that ocean, and calls it a risk multiplier for heatwaves, tropical cyclones and rainstorms. The agency says this El Niño could reach record strength by December."
  },
  {
   "id": "cta",
   "text": "Will this El Niño become the strongest on record? Yes or no?"
  }
 ],
